ULTRARAG ATTRIBUTION

Memory UltraRAG MCP serves UltraRAG's memory: one standing MEMORY.md per scope
plus one dated dialogue file per day, with the tools get_global_memory,
save_memory, get_local_memory, and save_local_memory.

The behaviour, the tool names and parameters, the file names, and the file
formats are UltraRAG's, taken from the upstream repository at a pinned revision:

  Upstream repository: https://github.com/OpenBMB/UltraRAG
  Upstream revision:   3a709a2aea3fbe46acca59c422621c94b6e86857
  Declared version:    0.3.0.2
  Reference file:      servers/memory/src/memory.py
  Upstream license:    Apache License 2.0

No UltraRAG source file is copied, vendored, or redistributed by this package.
The behaviour and the formats were reimplemented in original Python from that
reference, and this package's output is checked byte-for-byte against files the
upstream server at that revision produced, which are committed under
tests/fixtures/upstream/ with the method used to capture them.

THE DIFFERENCE THIS PACKAGE MAKES

Global memory keeps UltraRAG's own layout inside UltraRAG's UI storage tree:
<storage-root>/memory/<user_id>/MEMORY.md and .../project/<YYYY-MM-DD>.md.

Local memory is kept inside the project this server is bound to, at
<project-root>/.memory-rag/, in the same shape and format, so a project's memory
is private to that project and travels with it. The reference implementation keeps
every scope under one storage tree; this package moves the project scope into the
project and binds the project at startup. The remaining choices - the four-tool
surface, validating the roots before serving, and the browser view - are listed in
README.md and summarised below.

THE BROWSER VIEW

The optional browser view is built on ui-ultra-rag-mcp, an independent companion
project that provides reusable local UI infrastructure for UltraRAG-derived MCP
servers. It is not part of UltraRAG and carries no UltraRAG code; this package
pins it by commit and supplies a thin adapter that calls this server's own tools.

Memory is written from the page only through those tools, except for one write
that has no tool: replacing a whole standing document. That write is atomic and is
refused when the document changed since it was read, so it cannot silently drop a
write made by an agent. It is listed in README.md's choice table.

